# ============================================================================
# DevTrackAcademy Interview — environment
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Never commit .env.local.
# ============================================================================

# ── Supabase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<anon key>

# Service role. Bypasses RLS — server-side only, never exposed to the browser.
# The agent pipeline needs it to write blueprints, reports, skill_progress and
# agent_runs, and to read company_cache (which has RLS on and no policies).
# Supabase → Project Settings → API → service_role.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service role key>


# ── Code execution ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runs the candidate's coding submissions against the hidden tests.
# agentdesign.md §9.5: test_pass_rate comes from a real sandbox run, never from
# a model's opinion about whether the code works.
#
# Leave unset and the coding round still runs — the candidate writes and submits
# code and it is graded on approach — but there is no pass rate, which is 50% of
# the coding score. That half is reported as unavailable rather than guessed.
CODE_RUNNER=judge0

# Self-hosted (recommended for anything real):
#   JUDGE0_URL=http://your-host:2358
#   JUDGE0_AUTH_TOKEN=<optional, if you set one>
#
# Managed via RapidAPI (fastest to get going):
#   JUDGE0_URL=https://judge0-ce.p.rapidapi.com
#   JUDGE0_RAPIDAPI_KEY=<rapidapi key>
JUDGE0_URL=
JUDGE0_AUTH_TOKEN=
JUDGE0_RAPIDAPI_KEY=

# Judge0 language ids are NOT stable across versions. `GET /languages` on your
# instance is the authority; override any that drift:
#   JUDGE0_LANG_PYTHON=71
#   JUDGE0_LANG_JAVASCRIPT=63
#   JUDGE0_LANG_TYPESCRIPT=74
#   JUDGE0_LANG_JAVA=62
#   JUDGE0_LANG_CPP=54
#   JUDGE0_LANG_GO=60


# ── Razorpay ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Dashboard → Account & Settings → API Keys → Generate Key.
# Use rzp_test_* keys until you go live; the same code path handles both.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_xxxxxxxxxxxx
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=<key secret — server only, never expose>

# The SAME key id, exposed to the browser. Checkout needs it and it is public by
# design. The SECRET must never get a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_xxxxxxxxxxxx

# A DIFFERENT secret, set when you create the webhook:
# Dashboard → Account & Settings → Webhooks → Add New Webhook.
# Webhook URL:  https://<your-domain>/api/payments/webhook
# Active events: payment.captured, payment.failed, payment.authorized
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<webhook secret>


# ── Model providers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two are required. Prep and evaluation run on OpenAI; the live interviewer runs
# on Groq and does not follow AI_PROVIDER — see the note under it below.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...
# GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=...
# XAI_API_KEY=...

# ── The live interviewer is pinned to Groq ──────────────────────────────────
# Agent IV decides what to ask AND says it, in one call, inside the silence
# after the candidate stops talking. That silence is the whole of what makes a
# voice agent feel alive or dead, so time-to-first-token is the binding
# constraint and the provider IS the requirement rather than a preference.
#
# It therefore ignores AI_PROVIDER. Only AI_PROVIDER_IV overrides it, which is
# the escape hatch for measuring that claim rather than trusting it:
#   AI_PROVIDER_IV=openai       # A/B the whole premise
#   AI_MODEL_IV=openai/gpt-oss-20b   # smaller and faster; 120b is the default
#   AI_TIMEOUT_IV=2500          # falls back to a seed question past this


# ── Provider routing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THREE LEVELS, most specific wins:
#
#   1. AI_MODEL_<AGENT>     exact model for one agent   (beats everything)
#   2. AI_PROVIDER_<AGENT>  provider for one agent
#   3. AI_PROVIDER          provider for the whole system
#
# All three are read per request, so nothing needs a redeploy to move.

# Level 3 — the whole system in one switch. Values: openai | google | xai
AI_PROVIDER=openai

# Level 2 — move one agent to another provider. It keeps its tier, so it lands
# on that provider's equivalent model. Agent ids match agentdesign.md §1:
#   P1 P2 P3 P4 P5 P6 P7 SC · IV L2 L3 · E3 E4 E5 E6 SV · RI SP TR
#   AI_PROVIDER_P6=google       # blueprint on Gemini, everything else on OpenAI
#   AI_PROVIDER_E4=xai          # grading on Grok

# Level 1 — pin one agent to an exact model, bypassing the tier system entirely.
# Use this to A/B a model, or to buy quality on a single agent:
#   AI_MODEL_P6=gpt-5.6-sol     # blueprint on the frontier model
#   AI_MODEL_E4=gpt-5.6-terra   # grading on the mid tier
#   AI_MODEL_SV=gpt-5.6-sol     # skill-challenge review on the frontier model

# Per-agent timeout, in milliseconds. Worth tuning per deployment: the right
# value depends on how far the app sits from the provider, and that differs
# between a laptop and production. Only the live interviewer really matters —
# past its ceiling the turn speaks a pre-written seed question instead.
#   AI_TIMEOUT_IV=4000
#   AI_TIMEOUT_L3=2000

# ── Voice · Deepgram ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both legs, and it does NOT follow AI_PROVIDER. The language models are a
# routing choice; this is not, because it rests on a capability that is
# provider-specific and whose loss is silent rather than loud.
#
# nova-3 returns WORD-LEVEL TIMESTAMPS on the live socket, and E2's entire
# speech-metrics layer — pace, pause profile, filler rate, repetition — is
# arithmetic over them. A provider without them does not error; it produces a
# report with half the delivery panel dark. GET /api/ai/status reports
# `wordTimestampsAvailable` so the property can be checked rather than assumed.
#
# Console → Settings → API Keys. One key covers STT and TTS.
DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=

# Both legs stream:
#   STT  the BROWSER holds a WebSocket to Deepgram, so the transcript arrives
#        while the candidate is still talking rather than after they stop. The
#        key above never reaches the browser — /api/sessions/[id]/voice-token
#        mints a short-lived JWT with usage rights only.
#   TTS  /api/sessions/[id]/speak pipes Deepgram's response body straight to the
#        audio element, so the interviewer starts on the first MP3 frames.
#
# Overridable, but the defaults are the considered choice:
#   DEEPGRAM_STT_MODEL=nova-3
#   DEEPGRAM_TTS_MODEL=aura-2-thalia-en     # persona picks the voice per session
#
# NOTE ON PROSODY: Aura accepts no delivery direction. gpt-4o-mini-tts took one
# as prose, which is how L4's prosody (emotion, rate, emphasis) reached the
# audio. Voice selection is now the only delivery control, so L4's tone shapes
# the WORDS but not how they sound. `prosodyDirectionSupported` on
# /api/ai/status reports this; lib/ai/deepgram.ts carries the full note.

# ── The ladder (lib/ai/catalog.ts is the source of truth) ───────────────────
#            nano · fast          balanced              deep
#   openai   gpt-5.6-luna         gpt-5.6-luna          gpt-5.6-terra
#   google   gemini-3.5-flash-lite                      gemini-3.6-flash
#   xai      grok-build-0.1                             grok-4.3
#   groq     openai/gpt-oss-20b   openai/gpt-oss-120b   openai/gpt-oss-120b
#
# Three tiers resolve to the same OpenAI model on purpose — they still differ in
# timeout, temperature and retries, and they diverge on other providers. Only
# P6, P7 and SV use `deep`; the reasoning for every assignment is in lib/ai/config.ts.
#
# Voice models, Deepgram:  nova-3 (~$0.0077/min streaming, WORD timestamps)
#                          aura-2 (~$30/1M chars, no prosody direction)
#
# NOTE ON REASONING MODELS: the gpt-5.6 family reject `temperature` — the dial is
# `reasoningEffort` (none/minimal/low/medium/high/xhigh/max), set per agent in
# lib/ai/config.ts. Reasoning tokens are invisible in the response but billed as
# output tokens and cost real latency, which is why the two live-loop agents run
# at `none`. Gemini and Grok still honour temperature; run.ts sends whichever the
# active model actually supports.
#
# GET /api/ai/status shows live routing, prices, the active control (reasoning vs
# sampling) per agent, and voice capability.
